<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Lawrence River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St. Lawrence</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The St. Lawrence River flows from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> to the Atlantic in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. French explorers used it as a gateway to the interior, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> was founded on its banks. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> treat it as the axis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river.jpg" alt="St. Lawrence River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: abdallahh · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_fleuve_Saint-Laurent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 1710, the first empire stretched from Acadia along the <b>St. Lawrence</b> through the Great Lakes and down the Mississippi to Louisiana, covering some ten million square kilometres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France’s claim in North America began in 1534, when Jacques Cartier, sailing for Francis I, explored the Gulf of <b>St Lawrence</b> and planted a cross on the Gaspé Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Old Bering Sea stage, first described by Diamond Jenness from decorated ivory harpoon heads found on the <b>St. Lawrence</b> and Diomede Islands, ran from about 200 BCE to 500 CE; how it relates to the Okvik style is still debated.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thule-people">Thule people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Montreal's first railroad opened, running 12 km from Bonaventure Station in Montreal to the <b>St. Lawrence River</b> at Lachine to bypass the Lachine Rapids.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-19">19 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The eastern part, along the lower <b>St Lawrence</b> with Quebec City as capital, became Lower Canada, where the great majority of people were French-speaking Catholics living under French civil law and the seigneurial system.</blockquote><a href="/state/lower-canada">Lower Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>After the British conquest of New France in 1759 to 1760, the Treaty of Paris of 1763 ceded Canada to Britain, and the Royal Proclamation of that year organised the <b>St Lawrence</b> settlements as the Province of Quebec.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-quebec">Province of Quebec</a></li></ul></section>
